        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The seminal discovery of Toll and Toll-NF-#B signaling as a central mediator of innate immune responses in the model insect Drosophila catalyzed similar discoveries in other organisms including innate immunity in humans. These findings led to an appreciation of the fundamental roles of innate immunity in human immune deficiency on the one hand, and immune health of insects on the other. In Drosophila larvae, primed, but quiescent hematopoietic progenitors are called into action from an organ with functional similarities to mammalian bone marrow. Blood cells signal the fat body (equivalent to the mammalian liver) to activate the expression of antimicrobial peptide genes (humoral immunity). Antimicrobial peptides efficiently combat deadly bacteria and fungi. The Leptopilina wasps are natural metazoan enemies of Drosophila and their attack kills fruit flies. Wasp attack (e.g., L. boulardi) activates both the humoral and cellular immune arms of larval hosts, each being dependent on Toll-NF-#B signaling. Previous NASA-funded studies of Drosophila have shown that stresses in the space environment compromise hematopoietic development and modulate larval and adult innate immunity. We hypothesize that stimuli from the space environment will also alter the virulence of the parasitic wasps, and therefore their success on the fly hosts cultured in space.<p>

APPROACH:<br>
In Drosophila-wasp co-culture experiments using the NASA Fruit Fly Lab hardware over 30-days, we propose to (1) examine whether the fly or the wasp partner wins the arms race in space; (2) examine the effects of spaceflight on the anti-parasite response in fruit fly hosts; and (3) examine if virulence of “space-cultured” wasps is different from counterparts grown on Earth. While both L. boulardi and L. heterotoma parasites always succeed on the wild type, only the latter wasp species produces unique cell-killing activities residing in its virus-like particles. It also suppresses humoral immunity completely. We will examine if changes in the expression of VLP genes underlies changes in parasite virulence affected by spaceflight. Host defense and virulence will be inferred from a suite of other well-established assays of fly/host survival and measures of immune response.<p>

RESULTS:<br>
Experiment is currently in progress. Results will be available at the conclusion of the study.
        </div>

